[Event "Vienna"]
[Site "Vienna"]
[Date "1910"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Richard Reti"]
[Black "Savielly Tartakower"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B15"]
[Themes "opening-trap,queen-sacrifice,development"]
[Level "1"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Main Line"]
[Why "Twenty-one moves every club player should see once. White castles long so that a rook stands on the open d-file, Black takes a knight while his king is still on e8, and a queen sacrifice on d8 followed by a double check ends the game. The lesson is the first rule of the opening: a king left in the centre on an open file is a target, and a double check can only be answered by a king move."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Qd3 { Key: 5.Qd3 sets a trap more than it develops: the queen stands on the d-file, facing Black's queen on d8. The engine prefers 5.Nxf6+ (+0.4). } 5... e5 { Key: 5...e5 opens lines before Black has castled. 5...Nxe4 6.Qxe4 Qd5 was quieter (engine +0.2). } 6. dxe5 Qa5+ 7. Bd2 Qxe5 8. O-O-O { Key: 8.O-O-O: the rook arrives on the open d-file, which leads to d8, next to the black king. Development first, then the tactic. } 8... Nxe4 { Key: 8...Nxe4?? takes a knight and allows mate in three (engine M3). 8...Be7 was needed (engine +1.2). } 9. Qd8+ { Key: 9.Qd8+!! The queen sacrifice drags the king onto the d-file: 9...Kxd8 10.Bg5+ is a double check from bishop and rook, so only a king move is possible. } 9... Kxd8 10. Bg5+ Kc7 { Key: 10...Kc7 11.Bd8# or 10...Ke8 11.Rd8#: every flight square is covered. } 11. Bd8# 1-0

[Event "London"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "1862"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Augustus Mongredien"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B01"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,pawn-storm"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "Scandinavian Defense: Valencian Variation"]
[Why "Steinitz before his positional period. He plants a knight on e5, lifts a rook to h3 and pushes the g-pawn, and when Black opens the f-file himself with 15...fxg4 a rook sacrifice on h7 drags the king into the open. The king hunt that follows shows how queen, rook and bishop work together against a king with no pawn cover."]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qd8 4. d4 e6 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Bd3 Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. Be3 b6 9. Ne5 Bb7 10. f4 Nbd7 11. Qe2 Nd5 { Key: 11...Nd5 lets White exchange into a pawn on d5 that blocks Black's own bishop on b7. 11...c5 12.Rad1 cxd4 hit the centre first (engine -0.0 against +0.8). } 12. Nxd5 exd5 13. Rf3 { Key: 13.Rf3: the rook lift. The rook swings to h3 and aims down the h-file at the black king. } 13... f5 14. Rh3 g6 15. g4 fxg4 { Key: 15...fxg4? opens the f-file towards Black's own king. 15...Nxe5 16.fxe5 f4 kept the kingside closed (engine +0.4 against +2.6). } 16. Rxh7 { Key: 16.Rxh7! The rook is given to strip the king of its h-pawn. The engine slightly prefers 16.Qxg4 (+2.6 against +1.7), but both win because the h- and g-files are open. } 16... Nxe5 17. fxe5 Kxh7 18. Qxg4 Rg8 { Key: 18...Rg8 lets the queen in with check. 18...Qe8 was more stubborn (engine +2.8 against +3.3). } 19. Qh5+ Kg7 20. Qh6+ Kf7 21. Qh7+ { Key: 21.Qh7+ Ke6 22.Qh3+: the queen drives the king into the centre, where the rook on f1 joins with check (engine: mate in 8). } 21... Ke6 22. Qh3+ Kf7 23. Rf1+ { Key: 23.Rf1+ is strong, but 23.e6+ was faster (engine: mate in 6). } 23... Ke8 24. Qe6 Rg7 25. Bg5 Qd7 26. Bxg6+ Rxg6 27. Qxg6+ Kd8 28. Rf8+ Qe8 29. Qxe8# 1-0

[Event "St Petersburg 1895/96 (round 5)"]
[Site "St Petersburg"]
[Date "1895"]
[Round "5"]
[White "Mikhail Chigorin"]
[Black "Harry Nelson Pillsbury"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C60"]
[Themes "opposite-castling,pawn-storm,defence"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Fianchetto Defense"]
[Why "A pawn-storm race with the kings on opposite wings. Chigorin castles long and throws his g- and h-pawns forward; Pillsbury attacks with the a-, b- and c-pawns and is faster: the engine has Black about two pawns better at move 25. Then two king moves and one pawn grab, 35...Rxf2??, turn the game round. The defender who stays alive long enough gives the attacker time to go wrong."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 g6 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. d3 Nge7 6. Bg5 f6 7. Be3 a6 8. Ba4 b5 9. Bb3 Na5 10. Qd2 Nxb3 11. axb3 Bb7 12. Bh6 O-O 13. h4 d6 14. O-O-O { Key: 14.O-O-O: the kings stand on opposite wings. Now both sides storm with pawns: White's g- and h-pawns, Black's a-, b- and c-pawns. } 14... c5 15. g4 b4 16. Nb1 a5 17. Rdg1 a4 18. bxa4 Rxa4 { Key: 18...Rxa4: the a-file opens towards White's king first (engine -1.2). } 19. Qe3 Nc6 { Key: 19...Nc6 is slower than 19...Bxh6 20.Qxh6 c4 (engine -1.0 against -2.0). With the kings on opposite wings a tempo is worth more than a pawn. } 20. Bxg7 Kxg7 21. g5 Nd4 22. h5 Nxf3 23. hxg6 { Key: 23.hxg6: White ignores the knight on f3 and gives the rook on g1 to keep the pawn storm going. } 23... Nxg1 24. gxf6+ Kxf6 25. gxh7 Ke6 { Key: 25...Ke6? The king walks towards the centre; 25...Kf7 26.f4 Qf6 kept it safer (engine -2.8 against -1.2). } 26. Rxg1 Kd7 27. Qh3+ Kc6 28. Qe6 Ra8 29. Rg7 Kb6 { Key: 29...Kb6 throws away what is left of Black's edge: after 29...Qc8 the engine still had -1.1; now it says 0.00. } 30. Na3 Ba6 31. Rd7 Qxd7 32. Qxd7 Rad8 33. Qg7 bxa3 34. bxa3 c4 35. d4 Rxf2 { Key: 35...Rxf2?? takes a pawn and lets the h-pawn through: 36.h8=Q follows. 35...Rfe8 held the balance (engine +0.6 against +3.5). } 36. h8=Q Rxh8 37. Qxh8 Rf1+ 38. Kb2 exd4 39. Qxd4+ Kc7 40. a4 Rf7 41. a5 Kc8 42. Qxd6 Rb7+ 43. Kc3 Bb5 44. a6 Rc7 45. a7 1-0

[Event "Lodz (match)"]
[Site "Lodz"]
[Date "1908"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Akiba Rubinstein"]
[Black "Georg Salwe"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D33"]
[Themes "pawn-structure,blockade,positional"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Tarrasch Defense: Prague Variation"]
[Why "A model game against a weak c-pawn. After 9...bxc6 Black's c-pawn can never be defended by another pawn and the square in front of it, c5, can never be attacked by one. Rubinstein trades the bishop that guards c5, puts his knight and then a rook on that square, doubles on the c-file and wins the pawn with a small combination. The plan has three steps that every structure lesson repeats: fix the weak pawn, occupy the square in front of it, then attack it more times than it can be defended."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 c5 3. c4 e6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. g3 Nc6 7. Bg2 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Qb6 9. Nxc6 bxc6 { Key: 9.Nxc6 bxc6: Black's c- and d-pawns stand apart from the others. No black pawn can ever defend c6, and none can ever attack c5. } 10. O-O Be7 11. Na4 { Key: 11.Na4: the knight heads for c5, the square in front of the weak pawn. } 11... Qb5 12. Be3 O-O 13. Rc1 Bg4 14. f3 Be6 15. Bc5 { Key: 15.Bc5 offers to trade Black's dark-squared bishop; without it nothing in Black's camp can contest c5. } 15... Rfe8 16. Rf2 Nd7 17. Bxe7 Rxe7 18. Qd4 Ree8 19. Bf1 Rec8 20. e3 Qb7 21. Nc5 { Key: 21.Nc5 Nxc5 22.Rxc5: a rook replaces the knight on the blockade square and the c6 pawn is attacked again. } 21... Nxc5 22. Rxc5 Rc7 23. Rfc2 Qb6 24. b4 a6 25. Ra5 Rb8 { Key: 25...Rb8? 25...Rb7 26.a3 Qxd4 kept the damage small (engine +0.7 against +1.7). } 26. a3 Ra7 27. Rxc6 { Key: 27.Rxc6! Qxc6 28.Qxa7: a rook for a rook and the c-pawn. After the capture on c6 the black queen no longer guards a7. } 27... Qxc6 28. Qxa7 Ra8 29. Qc5 Qb7 30. Kf2 h5 31. Be2 g6 32. Qd6 Qc8 33. Rc5 Qb7 { Key: 33...Qb7 is passive; 33...Qe8 34.Rc7 Rc8 was the defence (engine +2.1 against +3.2). } 34. h4 a5 35. Rc7 Qb8 36. b5 a4 37. b6 Ra5 38. b7 1-0

[Event "Baden-Baden"]
[Site "Baden-Baden"]
[Date "1925"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Alexander Alekhine"]
[Black "Karel Treybal"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D67"]
[Themes "endgame,positional"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Orthodox Defense, Alekhine Variation"]
[Why "Alekhine trades queens on move 13 for a small edge (engine +0.4) and then plays a long ending with no forcing idea at all: the king walks to e2 and f3, the pawns take space, and two inaccuracies by Black on moves 29 and 30 hand White the exchange. An ending with equal material is still full of decisions, and the active king and rooks decide them."]

1. c4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Nbd7 5. e3 Be7 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Rc1 c6 8. Bd3 dxc4 9. Bxc4 Nd5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. Ne4 N7f6 12. Ng3 Qb4+ { Key: 12...Qb4+ offers the queen trade: engine +0.4. A small edge, but White's king can now walk to the centre. } 13. Qd2 Qxd2+ 14. Kxd2 { Key: 14.Kxd2: without queens the king is a fighting piece. It goes to e2 and then f3. } 14... Rd8 15. Rhd1 Bd7 16. Ne5 Be8 17. Ke2 Kf8 18. f4 { Key: 18.f4: White takes space with pawns while his king stands right behind them. } 18... g6 19. Kf3 Rac8 20. Bb3 Rc7 21. Ne2 Ne7 22. g4 Rdc8 23. Ng3 Nfd5 24. Ne4 Rd8 25. Nc5 b6 26. Na6 Rcc8 27. e4 f6 28. exd5 fxe5 29. d6 Rxd6 { Key: 29...Rxd6? 29...e4+ 30.Kxe4 Rxd6 gained a tempo with the pawn check (engine +0.7 against +1.6). } 30. fxe5 Rd5 { Key: 30...Rd5? 30...Rdd8 31.Bxe6 Bd7 kept Black in the game (engine +1.6 against +2.8). } 31. Bxd5 Nxd5 { Key: 31.Bxd5 Nxd5: White has won the exchange (engine +2.8). } 32. a3 g5 33. Nb4 Ne7 34. Nd3 Nd5 35. h4 gxh4 36. Nf4 Nxf4 37. Kxf4 Rd8 38. Kg5 Kg7 39. Kxh4 Rd5 40. Kg5 Rd8 41. Kf4 Rd7 42. Ke3 Rb7 43. b4 a6 44. Rf1 Ra7 45. Rf6 Re7 46. a4 Kg8 47. a5 b5 48. d5 exd5 49. e6 { Key: 48.d5 exd5 49.e6: a pawn break. The e-pawn runs while the black rook is busy on the queenside (engine +4.5). } 49... Kg7 50. g5 h5 51. Kd4 Rc7 52. Kc5 Rc8 53. Kb6 d4 54. e7 1-0

[Event "Ostend (round 8)"]
[Site "Ostend"]
[Date "1905"]
[Round "8"]
[White "Dawid Janowski"]
[Black "Siegbert Tarrasch"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D02"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,initiative"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Krause Variation"]
[Why "A race between a queenside pawn advance and a king attack. Tarrasch closes the queenside with 15...c4 and pushes his b-pawn to b4 to open lines there; it takes several moves, and at each step the engine prefers ...f5 to cover the king first. When the black knight and queen leave the kingside, Janowski sacrifices a knight on h6 and a rook on f7. A plan on one wing works only while the king on the other wing is safe."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 c5 3. c3 e6 4. Bf4 Qb6 5. Qb3 Nf6 6. e3 Nc6 7. h3 Be7 8. Nbd2 Bd7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O Rfc8 11. Ne5 Be8 12. Bg3 Nd7 13. Ndf3 Nf8 14. Rfd1 Na5 15. Qc2 c4 { Key: 15...c4 closes the queenside. Black's plan is now ...b5 and ...b4 against the pawn on c3. } 16. Nd2 f6 17. Nef3 Bg6 18. Qc1 h6 19. Nh2 Qd8 20. Bf3 b5 { Key: 20...b5 starts the pawn advance. 20...f5 first, to stop White's play on the kingside, was better (engine -0.4 against +0.5). } 21. e4 Nc6 22. exd5 exd5 23. Re1 b4 { Key: 23...b4 continues the plan, but it is slow. 23...f5 was still the engine's choice (+0.2 against +1.1). } 24. Ndf1 bxc3 25. bxc3 Qa5 26. Ne3 Bf7 27. Qd2 Ba3 28. Rab1 Nd7 { Key: 28...Nd7? The knight leaves the kingside; 28...Ne7 kept it covered (engine +1.0 against +2.2). } 29. Rb7 { Key: 29.Rb7: the rook takes the seventh rank and attacks the knight on d7. } 29... Nb6 30. Nf5 Qa6 { Key: 30...Qa6? The queen leaves the defence too. After 30...Rab8 White still has 31.Nxh6+ gxh6 (engine +3.7); now it is +5.0. } 31. Nxh6+ { Key: 31.Nxh6+! gxh6 32.Rxf7 Kxf7 33.Qxh6: knight and rook are given to tear the pawns from the black king. } 31... gxh6 32. Rxf7 Kxf7 33. Qxh6 Kg8 34. Qg6+ Kh8 35. Qxf6+ Kg8 36. Qg6+ Kh8 37. Re5 1-0

[Event "Karlsbad"]
[Site "Karlsbad"]
[Date "1911"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Akiba Rubinstein"]
[Black "Alexander Alekhine"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D15"]
[Themes "rook-endgame,endgame"]
[Level "5"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Süchting Variation"]
[Why "A rook ending that lasts more than forty moves and changes value several times by the engine's count. Rubinstein, the best rook-ending player of his time, keeps his rook active and his king next to the pawns; the young Alekhine twice takes a pawn when he should bring his king. The whole ending is Tarrasch's rule in practice: the king belongs in front of passed pawns and the rook belongs where it attacks."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. c4 c6 4. Nc3 Qb6 5. Qc2 Bg4 6. Bg5 Nbd7 7. e3 Ne4 { Key: 7...Ne4? An early knight jump that loses time; 7...Bf5 was level (engine +0.4 against +1.6). } 8. Bf4 e6 9. Bd3 Qa5 10. O-O Nxc3 11. bxc3 Bxf3 12. gxf3 dxc4 13. Bxc4 Nb6 14. Qb3 Rd8 15. Be2 Bd6 16. Bg3 Bxg3 17. hxg3 O-O 18. f4 c5 19. Bf3 Rd7 20. a4 Nd5 21. Rfc1 g6 22. Qb5 Qxb5 23. axb5 cxd4 24. cxd4 b6 25. Rc4 f5 26. Rc6 Kf7 27. Bxd5 exd5 28. Rac1 Rfd8 29. Kf1 Ke7 30. Ke2 Rd6 31. R6c3 R6d7 32. Kd3 Ra8 33. Rc6 Rd6 34. Ke2 { Key: 34.Ke2 lets Black equalise; 34.Rc7+ Rd7 35.Ke2 kept the rook on the seventh rank (engine +1.0 against 0.00). } 34... Rxc6 35. Rxc6 Kd7 36. f3 Re8 37. Kd3 Re7 38. g4 Re6 39. Rc1 Re7 40. Rh1 Ke6 41. Rc1 Kd7 42. Re1 Rf7 43. Ra1 Kd6 44. Rc1 Kd7 45. Rc6 Rf8 46. Ke2 Rf7 47. Kf2 Rf8 48. Kg3 Re8 49. Rc3 Re7 50. Kh4 h6 51. Kg3 h5 52. Kh4 Rh7 { Key: 52...Rh7? 52...hxg4 53.fxg4 Rh7+ was the defence (engine +1.3 against +2.5). } 53. Kg5 { Key: 53.Kg5 gives some of it back; 53.gxf5 gxf5 54.Kg5 was stronger (engine +2.5 against +1.3). In long rook endings both sides make mistakes. } 53... fxg4 54. fxg4 hxg4 55. Kxg4 Rh1 56. Kg5 Rb1 57. Ra3 Rxb5 { Key: 57...Rxb5? A pawn grab while the white king marches on. 57...Ke6 kept the king in front of the white pawns (engine +0.6 against +2.3). } 58. Rxa7+ Kd6 59. Kxg6 Rb3 60. f5 Rxe3 { Key: 60...Rxe3: engine +3.3. The white king escorts the f-pawn and the black king is far away on the queenside. } 61. f6 Rg3+ 62. Kh7 Rf3 63. f7 Rf4 64. Kg7 Rg4+ 65. Kf6 Rf4+ 66. Kg5 Rf1 67. Kg6 Rg1+ 68. Kf6 Rf1+ 69. Kg7 Rg1+ 70. Kf8 Rd1 71. Ke8 Re1+ 72. Kd8 Rf1 73. Rd7+ Kc6 74. Ke8 { Key: 74.Ke8 brings the king beside the f7 pawn. The engine prefers 74.Re7 (+6.7 against +4.9); both win because the black king is cut off. } 74... Rf4 75. Re7 Kb5 76. Rc7 1-0

[Event "Match, New York, game 16"]
[Site "New York"]
[Date "1909"]
[Round "16"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Frank Marshall"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "C49"]
[Themes "opposite-bishops,fortress,defence"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Four Knights Game: Spanish Variation, Janowski Variation"]
[Why "Capablanca, usually the one who squeezes, spends this game defending. Two inaccuracies (11.Ba3 and 17.Nd2) give Marshall an edge of up to two pawns by the engine. Capablanca trades into an ending with bishops of opposite colours, where a pawn less does not matter: the defender blockades on the squares of his own bishop's colour, which the other bishop can never contest."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bb5 Bb4 5. O-O O-O 6. d3 Bxc3 7. bxc3 d6 8. Re1 Ne7 9. d4 c6 10. dxe5 dxe5 11. Ba3 { Key: 11.Ba3? 11.Qxd8 Rxd8 12.Bf1 was level (engine +0.5); now Black is better (-0.5). } 11... cxb5 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 13. Bxe7 Re8 14. Bd6 Nd7 15. Red1 f6 16. Rab1 a6 17. Nd2 { Key: 17.Nd2? 17.c4 was needed (engine -0.2 against -1.4). White's queenside pawns become targets. } 17... Nb6 18. c4 Na4 19. Re1 Rd8 20. c5 Nxc5 21. Bxc5 Rxd2 22. Rb2 Be6 23. Be3 Rd7 24. f3 Rc8 25. Rc1 Rc3 26. Kf2 Rdc7 27. Bd2 Ra3 28. Ra1 Rc4 29. Bb4 Ra4 30. Bd2 f5 { Key: 30...f5: engine -1.4. Black is better on every count; Capablanca's task is now defence. } 31. exf5 Bxf5 32. c3 e4 33. fxe4 Bxe4 34. a3 Bc6 35. Rb4 Rc5 36. Rxa4 bxa4 37. Rb1 Rf5+ 38. Kg1 Rd5 39. Rb2 Rd3 40. Kf2 Bd5 41. Be1 Kf7 42. Rd2 Rxd2+ { Key: 42...Rxd2+ 43.Bxd2: only bishops of opposite colours are left. Black is a pawn up and the engine already says 0.00. } 43. Bxd2 Ke6 44. g3 Ba2 45. Bf4 Kd5 46. Ke3 Kc4 47. Kd2 Kb3 48. Bd6 Bb1 49. h4 Bf5 50. Bf8 b5 { Key: To advance, Black's pawns must cross dark squares, which White's bishop and pawns guard. Black's bishop moves on light squares and can never contest them: a fortress. } 51. Bd6 a5 52. Bf8 g6 53. Be7 b4 54. cxb4 Kxa3 55. bxa5+ Kb3 56. a6 Kc4 1/2-1/2

[Event "World Championship match, game 12"]
[Site "Germany"]
[Date "1908"]
[Round "12"]
[White "Siegbert Tarrasch"]
[Black "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C49"]
[Themes "knight-vs-bishop,endgame,world-championship"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Four Knights Game: Spanish Variation, Double Spanish"]
[Why "Tarrasch gets a bishop against a knight with pawns on both wings, and Lasker drifts into a passive defence: the engine has White more than a pawn ahead from move 24. The bishop covers both wings at once; the knight can only be on one. Both players err late in the ending (62.Kb6 and 62...Nf5 each swing the engine by more than two pawns): calculate even when the position looks won."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. O-O d6 { Key: 5...d6? An early inaccuracy: 5...O-O 6.d3 Bxc3 was level (engine +0.4 against +1.4). } 6. Nd5 Bc5 7. d4 exd4 8. Nxd4 Bxd4 9. Qxd4 O-O 10. Nxf6+ Qxf6 11. Qxf6 gxf6 { Key: 11.Qxf6 gxf6: queens off and Black's kingside pawns are doubled. The ending favours White. } 12. Bh6 Re8 13. Rfe1 a6 14. Bf1 Kh8 15. Bd2 Ne7 16. Bc3 Ng8 17. f4 Kg7 18. Re3 Kf8 19. Bd3 Bd7 20. Rae1 Bb5 21. e5 Bxd3 { Key: 21...Bxd3 22.Rxd3: Black gives up his last bishop. From here it is bishop against knight. } 22. Rxd3 fxe5 23. fxe5 dxe5 24. Rxe5 b6 { Key: 24...b6: engine +1.2. Bishop against knight with pawns on both wings: the long-range piece is the better one. } 25. Rh5 h6 26. Bd2 Re6 27. Rd7 Rc8 28. Rf5 Re7 29. Rxe7 Kxe7 30. Re5+ Kf6 31. Bc3 Kg6 32. Re3 Rd8 33. Rg3+ Kf5 34. Rg7 Ke6 35. Rh7 c5 36. Kf2 b5 37. Ke2 b4 38. Bd2 Rd4 39. g3 Rg4 40. Bxh6 Nf6 41. Rh8 Rc4 42. Kd1 Ng4 43. Bf4 Kf5 44. b3 Rc3 45. Bd2 Rf3 46. Rh5+ Ke4 47. Rh4 Kf5 48. h3 Nf6 49. Rf4+ Rxf4 50. Bxf4 { Key: 49.Rf4+ Rxf4 50.Bxf4: the last rooks come off. The bishop guards both wings; the knight can only be on one (engine +2.6). } 50... Ke4 51. Ke2 c4 52. Bg5 Nd5 53. bxc4 Nc3+ 54. Kd2 Kf5 55. Bf4 Nxa2 56. c5 Ke6 57. c6 Nc3 58. Kd3 Nd5 59. Kc4 Ne7 60. Kc5 a5 61. c7 Kd7 62. Kb6 { Key: 62.Kb6? lets Black back in; 62.h4 f5 63.Kb5 kept a won game (engine +6.1 against +3.5). } 62... Nf5 { Key: 62...Nf5? returns the favour; 62...a4 was the defence (engine +3.5 against +5.9). } 63. Kxa5 Nd4 64. Kxb4 Nxc2+ 65. Kc4 1-0

[Event "Moscow"]
[Site "Moscow"]
[Date "1925"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Savielly Tartakower"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D30"]
[Themes "endgame,exchange-sacrifice,positional"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Capablanca Variation"]
[Why "Tartakower's bishop on a2 is trapped, so he gives a rook for it and a pawn (29...Rxd4), an exchange sacrifice made to save material rather than to attack. Capablanca then converts rook against bishop the way endgame books describe it: rooks on the seventh rank, pawns kept on both wings so the bishop cannot guard them all, and the king walked forward. There is no combination; the engine's number rises slowly from +2 to +8."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Nbd7 5. e3 c6 6. Nbd2 Qa5 7. cxd5 exd5 8. Bd3 Ne4 9. O-O Nxg5 10. Nxg5 Nf6 11. h3 h6 12. Ngf3 Bd6 13. Ne5 O-O 14. f4 c5 15. Nb3 Qb6 16. dxc5 Bxc5 17. Nxc5 Qxc5 18. Qd2 Nd7 19. Rac1 Qd6 20. Nf3 Nc5 21. Bb1 f5 22. Rfd1 Be6 23. Nd4 Ne4 24. Bxe4 dxe4 { Key: 24.Bxe4 dxe4: White gives his bishop for the knight; Black's pawn on e4 is now cut off from its neighbours (engine +1.6). } 25. Qa5 Qd5 26. Rc5 Qxa2 27. Qxa2 Bxa2 28. b3 Rfd8 29. Ra1 Rxd4 { Key: 29...Rxd4 30.exd4 Bxb3: an exchange sacrifice. After 28.b3 and 29.Ra1 the bishop on a2 had no way out, so Black gives a rook for knight and pawn and frees it. } 30. exd4 Bxb3 31. Rb5 Bc4 32. Rxb7 a5 33. Rc1 Be6 34. Rcc7 Rd8 { Key: 34...Rd8? 34...g5 35.fxg5 hxg5 kept the kingside pawns together (engine +2.4 against +3.8). } 35. Rxg7+ { Key: 35.Rxg7+: two rooks on the seventh rank collect pawns. } 35... Kf8 36. Rg6 Bc4 37. Rc6 Ba2 38. Rf6+ Kg8 39. Ra6 e3 40. Re7 e2 41. Rg6+ Kf8 42. Rxe2 Bf7 43. Rf6 Rxd4 44. Re5 Rxf4 45. Rxa5 Kg7 46. Raxf5 Rxf5 47. Rxf5 Bg6 48. Ra5 Be4 { Key: 48...Be4: rook and two pawns against bishop and a pawn (engine +4.6). The rook needs a target; the plan is a passed pawn supported by the king. } 49. Ra6 Bc2 50. Kf2 Be4 51. g4 Bc2 52. Kg3 Bb3 53. Kf4 Bc2 54. h4 Bb1 55. h5 { Key: 55.h5 is slower than 55.Ke5 Bc2 56.Rc6 (engine +4.8 against +3.6), but White keeps the win. } 55... Bc2 56. Ke5 Bb1 57. Ra7+ Kh8 58. Rb7 Bd3 59. Rb3 Bc2 60. Rb2 Bd3 61. Kd4 Bf1 62. Ke4 Kg7 63. Rd2 Bb5 64. Ke5 Ba4 65. Rb2 Bc6 66. Rb4 Bf3 67. Rc4 Be2 68. Rd4 Bb5 69. Rd5 Be2 70. Kf5 Bf3 71. Rd7+ Kf8 72. Kf6 Ke8 73. Rd4 1-0

[Event "World Championship match, game 1"]
[Site "New York"]
