[Opening "Four Knights Game: Spanish Variation, Symmetrical Variation"]
[Why "Two bishops against two knights for forty moves. While the position is closed the knights hold (the engine is near 0.00 around move 24); Janowski prepares the opening of the kingside, and once the files open the bishops outrank the knights. At the end he even gives the pair back for a passed pawn. The bishop pair is a long-term advantage: keep it, and open the position when the time is right."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Bb4 5. O-O O-O 6. d3 d6 7. Bg5 Bxc3 8. bxc3 h6 9. Bh4 Bg4 10. h3 Bxf3 { Key: 10...Bxf3 11.Qxf3: Black has now given both bishops for knights. White has the two bishops. } 11. Qxf3 g5 12. Bg3 Nh7 { Key: 12...Nh7: the knight retreats to the edge; 12...a6 13.Bxc6 bxc6 was simpler (engine +0.5 against +0.9). } 13. Rab1 Qc8 14. Rfd1 Kg7 15. d4 f6 16. Be2 Ne7 17. Qd3 Rb8 18. Bg4 Qe8 19. Qc4 h5 20. Be6 Qc6 21. Qd3 h4 22. Bh2 Rfd8 23. d5 Qa4 24. g3 Ng6 { Key: 24...Ng6: 24...hxg3 25.Bxg3 was better (engine -0.5 against +0.2). Black had chances while the position stayed closed. } 25. Rb4 Qe8 26. Rdb1 b6 27. Qa6 Nhf8 28. Bg4 hxg3 29. fxg3 { Key: 28...hxg3 29.fxg3: the f-file opens. Open lines are what bishops need. } 29... Nd7 30. Qxa7 Rdc8 31. Qa6 Nc5 32. Qe2 Rd8 33. Rf1 Ra8 34. h4 gxh4 35. gxh4 Qf7 36. h5 Nf8 37. Bg3 Kh8 38. Bh4 Nh7 39. Bf5 Rg8+ 40. Kh1 Raf8 41. Rbb1 Qe7 42. Rg1 Rxg1+ { Key: 42...Rxg1+: engine +1.8. The kingside is open and the bishops come alive. } 43. Rxg1 Rg8 44. Rg6 Rxg6 45. hxg6 Ng5 46. Qh5+ Kg8 47. Bxg5 fxg5 48. Be6+ { Key: 47.Bxg5 fxg5 48.Be6+: White gives the bishop pair back at the right moment, for a passed pawn on e6 (engine +5.2). } 48... Nxe6 49. dxe6 Qf6 { Key: 49...Qf6? 49...Qg7 was more stubborn (engine +6.0 against +7.3). } 50. Qh7+ Kf8 51. Kg2 g4 52. Qf7+ Qxf7 53. gxf7 d5 54. Kg3 1-0

[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York"]
