[Themes "iqp,attack"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "English Opening: Anglo-Indian Defense, Scandinavian Defense, Exchange Variation"]
[Why "How to play against an isolated queen's pawn: blockade it, then use the squares around it. Marshall plants a knight on d5 in front of White's pawn on d4, and when Reti sends his queen pawn hunting on the queenside the knight jumps to f4 and the attack on the white king follows. The turning point is 20.Qxc7, a pawn grab with the queen far from her king (engine -1.1 to -3.5)."]

1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 d5 3. cxd5 Nxd5 4. d4 Bf5 5. Nc3 e6 6. Qb3 Nc6 7. e4 Nxc3 8. exf5 Nd5 9. Bb5 { Key: 9.Bb5 lets Black finish development; 9.Bd2 Qd6 10.Rc1 kept White ahead (engine +1.4 against +0.2). } 9... Bb4+ 10. Bd2 Bxd2+ 11. Nxd2 exf5 { Key: 11...exf5: White's d4 pawn stands alone. No c- or e-pawn is left to defend it: an isolated queen's pawn. } 12. Bxc6+ bxc6 { Key: Black's knight on d5 blockades the isolated pawn: the pawn cannot advance, and no white pawn can chase the knight away. } 13. O-O O-O 14. Qa4 Rb8 15. Nb3 Rb6 16. Qxa7 { Key: 16.Qxa7: the white queen goes pawn hunting on the queenside while Black's pieces turn to the king. } 16... Qg5 17. Qa5 c5 18. Qxc5 Nf4 { Key: 18...Nf4: the blockading knight jumps into the attack (engine -2.7). } 19. g3 Rh6 20. Qxc7 { Key: 20.Qxc7? Another pawn, with the queen far from her king; 20.Qc2 was the defence (engine -1.1 against -3.5). } 20... Ne2+ 21. Kg2 Qg4 22. Rh1 f4 { Key: 22...f4 keeps attacking; 22...Qh3+ 23.Kf3 was even stronger (engine -4.7 against -3.4). } 23. f3 Qh3+ 24. Kf2 Rc8 25. Qa5 Nxg3 26. Rhg1 Qxh2+ 27. Rg2 Qh4 28. Rc1 Re8 29. Qb5 Ne4+ 30. Kf1 Qh1+ 0-1

