[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Taimanov Variation, Szén Variation"]
[Why "Morphy against the strongest German player of his time. Anderssen plays 7...f5 before he has developed, Morphy answers with knight jumps to d5 and c7, and the black king ends on f6 in the middle of the board. Thirty-three moves that show why development comes before pawn storms, and how a king without shelter loses to pieces that are already in play."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nb5 d6 6. Bf4 e5 7. Be3 f5 { Key: 7...f5? Black opens his king's diagonal before developing a piece; 7...Nf6 was level (engine +0.1 against +1.0). } 8. N1c3 f4 9. Nd5 fxe3 10. Nbc7+ { Key: 10.Nbc7+: the knight forks the king and the rook on a8. The king must move and can no longer castle. } 10... Kf7 11. Qf3+ { Key: 11.Qf3+: Morphy keeps the king in the open instead of taking the rook at once. The engine prefers 11.Nxa8, but the attack is the practical choice. } 11... Nf6 12. Bc4 Nd4 13. Nxf6+ d5 14. Bxd5+ Kg6 { Key: 14...Kg6? walks into the queen check. 14...Ke7 15.Qh5 gxf6 held (engine +0.1 against +2.6). } 15. Qh5+ { Key: 15.Qh5+ Kxf6 16.fxe3: the black king stands in the middle of the board with every white piece near it. } 15... Kxf6 16. fxe3 Nxc2+ { Key: 16...Nxc2+ takes a pawn with check, but 16...g6 was the only defence (engine +3.3 against +5.0). } 17. Ke2 1-0

