[Opening "Italian Game: Evans Gambit, Paulsen Variation"]
[Why "Anderssen, the attacking player of the romantic era, against the young Zukertort. White gives a pawn in the Evans Gambit for time, opens the g-file with his pawns and doubles rooks on it. When Black misjudges the defence (28...Qd6 allows mate in seven by the engine) the finish is a classic: a bishop sacrifice on h7 and a rook mate on h8."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. d4 exd4 { Key: 6...exd4: engine -0.1. The gambit pawn buys White time for development and the centre. } 7. O-O Bb6 8. cxd4 d6 9. d5 Na5 10. Bb2 Ne7 11. Bd3 O-O 12. Nc3 Ng6 13. Ne2 c5 14. Qd2 f6 15. Kh1 Bc7 16. Rac1 Rb8 17. Ng3 b5 18. Nf5 b4 19. Rg1 Bb6 20. g4 Ne5 21. Bxe5 dxe5 22. Rg3 Rf7 23. g5 Bxf5 { Key: 23...Bxf5? Black takes on f5 and opens lines near his own king; 23...c4 was the engine's choice (-0.5 against +0.6). } 24. exf5 Qxd5 25. gxf6 Rd8 26. Rcg1 Kh8 { Key: 26...Kh8? 26...Rxf6 27.Rxg7+ Kh8 was more stubborn (engine +3.1 against +4.7). } 27. fxg7+ Kg8 28. Qh6 Qd6 { Key: 28...Qd6?? allows a forced mate (engine: mate in 7); 28...e4 was the only try (+4.9). } 29. Qxh7+ Kxh7 30. f6+ Kg8 { Key: 30...Kg8 31.Bh7+ Kxh7 32.Rh3+ Kg8 33.Rh8#: the bishop sacrifice drags the king back onto the rook's file. } 31. Bh7+ Kxh7 32. Rh3+ Kg8 33. Rh8# 1-0

