[Why "An attack with the kings on opposite wings. Alekhine castles long and drives his e- and f-pawns at Marshall's king. One passive knight move, 20...Nd5, lets the f-pawn break through to f6, and the pawn becomes a battering ram against g7. Pawn storms work when your own king is on the other wing: every pawn you push is an attacker, not a weakness."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 Nf6 3. cxd5 Nxd5 4. e4 Nf6 5. Bd3 e5 6. dxe5 Ng4 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. Bg5 Be7 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nc3 Ncxe5 11. Nxe5 Qxe5 12. h3 Nf6 13. Qd2 Bd7 14. Qe3 Bc6 15. O-O-O O-O { Key: 15.O-O-O O-O: the kings are on opposite wings. } 16. f4 { Key: 16.f4, then e5 and f5: White's pawns advance on the wing where Black's king lives, far from White's own king. } 16... Qe6 17. e5 Rfe8 18. Rhe1 Rad8 { Key: 18...Rad8: engine +1.4. With the kings on opposite wings pawns can advance without exposing their own king. } 19. f5 Qe7 20. Qg5 Nd5 { Key: 20...Nd5? The knight blocks the d-file but lets the f-pawn through; 20...Qf8 21.Qh4 kept the damage small (engine +1.0 against +3.3). } 21. f6 { Key: 21.f6! The pawn breaks into the king's shelter. If 21...Nxf6 22.exf6 Qxf6 23.Qxf6 gxf6 Black's kingside is wrecked. } 21... Qf8 22. Bc4 Nxc3 23. Rxd8 Rxd8 24. fxg7 Nxa2+ { Key: 24...Nxa2+ grabs a pawn with check, but 24...Qe8 was the last defence (engine +3.5 against +6.1). } 25. Kb1 Qe8 26. e6 Be4+ 27. Ka1 f5 28. e7+ Rd5 29. Qf6 { Key: 29.Qf6, and mate cannot be stopped (engine: mate in 3). } 29... Qf7 30. e8=Q+ 1-0

