[Why "Nimzowitsch takes the e-file, then sends his knight on a long road (h1, f2, h3, g5) and his queen around the kingside until Black's position gives way. Rubinstein defends well for twenty moves; one bishop move, 26...Bb6, costs three pawns by the engine, and the reply 27.Qh4 gives most of it back. The game shows slow pressure on one file and one wing, and how small errors swing a tense position."]

1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nb4 6. Bc4 e6 7. O-O N8c6 8. d3 Nd4 9. Nxd4 cxd4 10. Ne2 a6 11. Ng3 Bd6 12. f4 { Key: 12.f4: White takes space on the kingside; the knight on g3 and the queen support it. } 12... O-O 13. Qf3 Kh8 14. Bd2 f5 15. Rae1 Nc6 16. Re2 Qc7 17. exf5 exf5 { Key: 17.exf5 exf5: the e-file opens and White takes it at once with Re2 and Rfe1. } 18. Nh1 Bd7 { Key: 18...Bd7: engine +0.3. White's knight starts the long road h1, f2, h3 to reach g5. } 19. Nf2 Rae8 20. Rfe1 Rxe2 { Key: 20.Rfe1 Rxe2 21.Rxe2: White owns the e-file. } 21. Rxe2 Nd8 22. Nh3 Bc6 23. Qh5 g6 24. Qh4 Kg7 25. Qf2 Bc5 26. b4 Bb6 { Key: 26...Bb6? 26...Be7 was the defence (engine +0.6 against +3.7). } 27. Qh4 { Key: 27.Qh4 misses 27.Qe1, taking the e-file with the queen (engine +3.7 against +0.7). } 27... Re8 28. Re5 Nf7 29. Bxf7 Qxf7 { Key: 29.Bxf7 Qxf7 30.Ng5: the knight arrives on g5 at last and hits the queen. } 30. Ng5 Qg8 31. Rxe8 Bxe8 32. Qe1 Bc6 { Key: 32...Bc6? 32...h5 33.Qe7+ was more stubborn (engine +3.0 against +4.8). } 33. Qe7+ Kh8 34. b5 Qg7 35. Qxg7+ Kxg7 36. bxc6 { Key: 35.Qxg7+ Kxg7 36.bxc6: White has won a piece (engine +4.6). } 36... bxc6 37. Nf3 c5 38. Ne5 Bc7 39. Nc4 Kf7 40. g3 Bd8 41. Ba5 Be7 42. Bc7 Ke6 43. Nb6 h6 44. h4 g5 45. h5 g4 46. Be5 1-0

