1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bg5 O-O 6. e3 Nbd7 7. Rc1 c6 8. Qc2 Re8 9. Bd3 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Nd5 11. Ne4 f5 { Key: 11...f5? The pawn weakens e5 for good; 11...Qa5+ 12.Kf1 was level (engine -0.2 against +1.1). } 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Ned2 b5 14. Bxd5 cxd5 { Key: 14.Bxd5 cxd5: White trades his light-squared bishop. Black's pawns on d5, e6 and f5 stand on light squares, so his remaining bishop has little to do. } 15. O-O a5 16. Nb3 a4 17. Nc5 { Key: 17.Nc5: the knight lands on c5, a square no black pawn can ever attack. } 17... Nxc5 18. Qxc5 Qxc5 19. Rxc5 b4 20. Rfc1 Ba6 21. Ne5 { Key: 21.Ne5: a second outpost. With Black's d- and f-pawns already past the sixth rank, nothing can drive the knight away. } 21... Reb8 22. f3 b3 23. a3 h6 24. Kf2 Kh7 { Key: 24...Kh7: engine +2.7. The white king walks to the centre by f2, g3 and f4. } 25. h4 Rf8 26. Kg3 Rfb8 27. Rc7 Bb5 28. R1c5 Ba6 29. R5c6 Re8 30. Kf4 Kg8 31. h5 Bf1 32. g3 Ba6 { Key: 32...Ba6? 32...Bb5 kept the bishop more active (engine +2.5 against +3.7). } 33. Rf7 Kh7 34. Rcc7 Rg8 35. Nd7 Kh8 36. Nf6 Rgf8 { Key: 36...Rgf8?? loses material at once; 36...Raf8 was necessary (engine +4.4 against +12.4). } 37. Rxg7 Rxf6 38. Ke5 1-0

