[White "Rudolf Spielmann"]
[Black "Akiba Rubinstein"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C90"]
[Themes "rook-endgame,endgame"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Closed"]
[Why "Rubinstein's rook endings are the classic models of the active rook, and this is one of them. Spielmann wins a pawn in the middlegame at the cost of his king's cover; Rubinstein wins it back, trades queens, puts his rook on the second rank and walks his king into the white position. The ending turns on activity, not material."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 Bg4 9. h3 Bh5 10. d3 O-O 11. Nbd2 d5 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Nf1 Bf6 14. g4 Bg6 15. g5 Be7 16. Nxe5 Nxe5 17. Rxe5 { Key: 16.Nxe5 Nxe5 17.Rxe5: White wins a pawn, but his pawns on g5 and h3 have left his king open. } 17... Nb6 18. d4 Nd7 19. Re1 Bxg5 { Key: 19...Bxg5 20.Bxg5 Qxg5+: the pawn comes back with check. } 20. Bxg5 Qxg5+ 21. Qg4 Qd8 22. Ng3 Nf6 23. Qf3 Qd7 24. Kh2 a5 25. a3 Rab8 26. Re5 Rfe8 27. Rg1 b4 28. Rxa5 bxc3 29. Qxc3 Ne4 { Key: 29...Ne4 gives White an edge; 29...Qd6 was level (engine -0.2 against +0.8). } 30. Nxe4 Rxe4 31. Rd5 Qe7 32. Rc5 Re2 33. Qg3 Qd6 34. Qxd6 cxd6 { Key: 33...Qd6 34.Qxd6 cxd6: queens off. Black's rook is about to become the more active one. } 35. Rc7 Rxb2 { Key: 35...Rxb2: the black rook reaches White's second rank. } 36. Rgc1 Kf8 37. Bc2 Ra2 38. Bxg6 hxg6 39. R1c2 Rxc2 40. Rxc2 Ra8 { Key: 39...Rxc2 40.Rxc2 Ra8: a rook ending, four pawns each. Black's king is nearer the centre and his rook is about to go to a4. } 41. Rc3 Ra4 42. Rd3 Ke7 43. Kg3 Ke6 44. Kf3 Kd5 45. Ke2 g5 46. Rb3 f6 47. Ke3 Kc4 48. Rd3 d5 49. Kd2 Ra8 50. Kc2 Ra7 51. Kd2 Re7 52. Rc3+ Kxd4 53. a4 Ra7 54. Ra3 Ra5 55. Ra1 Kc4 56. Ke3 { Key: 56.Ke3 helps Black; 56.Rc1+ Kb4 57.Rb1+ drove the king back (engine -0.5 against -1.3). An active rook gives checks. } 56... d4+ 57. Kd2 Rf5 58. Ke1 Kb4 59. Ke2 Ka5 60. Ra3 Rf4 61. Ra2 Rh4 62. Kd3 Rxh3+ 63. Kxd4 Rh4+ 64. Kd3 Rxa4 65. Re2 Rf4 66. Ke3 Kb6 67. Rc2 Kb7 68. Rc1 Ra4 69. Rh1 Kc6 70. Rh7 Ra7 71. Ke4 Kd6 72. Kf5 g6+ 73. Kxg6 Rxh7 74. Kxh7 { Key: 74.Kxh7 loses faster; 74.Kxf6 g4 75.Kg5 was the try (engine -4.4 against -8.0). } 74... Ke5 75. Kg6 g4 0-1

[Event "Match, Berlin"]
[Site "Berlin"]
[Date "1918"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "Siegbert Tarrasch"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D34"]
[Themes "opposite-bishops,initiative,endgame"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Tarrasch Defense: Classical Variation"]
[Why "Bishops of opposite colours with queens and rooks still on the board favour the attacker, because the defender's bishop cannot contest the squares the other bishop attacks. Lasker wins a pawn on move 16, keeps the pressure for thirty moves and then breaks through on the queenside: 51.b6 axb6 52.a6 puts a black pawn on b6 in the way of Black's own bishop."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 c5 3. c4 e6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. g3 Nf6 7. Bg2 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. Bg5 d4 11. Ne4 Be7 12. Bxf6 Bxf6 13. Rc1 Re8 14. Ne1 Bf5 15. Nc5 Bc8 16. Nxb7 Bxb7 17. Rxc6 { Key: 16.Nxb7 Bxb7 17.Rxc6: White wins the b-pawn. } 17... Qa5 18. a3 Rab8 19. Rc2 Ba6 20. Nd3 Bxd3 { Key: 20...Bxd3? 20...Rbc8 kept more (engine +0.9 against +1.8). After 21.Qxd3 the bishops are of opposite colours. } 21. Qxd3 Rec8 { Key: 21.Qxd3: White's light-squared bishop has no rival; Black's dark-squared bishop cannot defend a single light square. } 22. Rfc1 Rxc2 23. Rxc2 h6 24. Be4 Re8 25. b4 Qe5 26. Bf3 Bd8 27. Rc5 Qe7 28. Rb5 Qf6 29. Rf5 Qb6 30. Rb5 Qf6 31. a4 Qd6 32. Qc4 Bf6 33. Bd5 Re7 34. Rc5 Rd7 35. a5 g6 36. h4 Be5 37. Kg2 Kg7 38. Be4 Qe7 39. Bc6 Rd8 40. Bd5 Qf6 41. Rc6 Qf5 42. Rc5 Qf6 43. Bf3 d3 44. exd3 Bd4 45. Rc6 Qe5 46. Rc7 Qf6 47. b5 g5 48. hxg5 hxg5 { Key: 47.b5 g5 48.hxg5 hxg5: Black's counterplay opens the h-file but comes too late (engine +3.0). } 49. Qa2 Rh8 50. Bd5 Rf8 51. b6 { Key: 51.b6! axb6 52.a6: the black pawn on b6 now blocks the bishop's road to a7, and the queening square a8 is light. The a-pawn runs. } 51... axb6 52. a6 g4 53. a7 Qh6 54. Kf1 Qd6 55. Rxf7+ { Key: 55.Rxf7+ Rxf7 56.Bxf7: the rook clears the way and the a-pawn queens. } 55... Rxf7 56. Bxf7 1-0

[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York"]
[Date "1924"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Efim Bogoljubov"]
