[Level "3"]
[Opening "French Defense: McCutcheon Variation, Bogoljubow Variation"]
[Why "Queens come off on move 12 and Capablanca plays for small things: he takes away the bishop pair, then trades into knight against bishop where Black's e-pawn is isolated and falls to the knight. The knight then escorts a passed g-pawn. The engine's edge for White stays around one pawn for thirty moves; technique turns it into a win."]

1. d4 e6 2. e4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Bb4 5. exd5 Qxd5 6. Bxf6 gxf6 { Key: 6.Bxf6 gxf6: Black gets the bishop pair but doubled f-pawns. } 7. Qd2 Qa5 8. Nge2 Nd7 9. Nc1 Nb6 10. Nb3 Qg5 11. a3 Qxd2+ { Key: 11...Qxd2+ 12.Kxd2: queens off on move 12. } 12. Kxd2 Be7 13. Bb5+ c6 14. Bd3 Bd7 15. Nc5 O-O-O 16. Nxd7 Rxd7 { Key: 16.Nxd7 Rxd7: White removes one of the two bishops; now each side has a bishop and a knight. } 17. Ne2 c5 18. dxc5 Bxc5 19. Ke1 f5 20. Rg1 h5 21. g3 Nd5 22. Bb5 Rdd8 23. Rd1 a6 24. Bc4 Nf6 25. Rxd8+ Rxd8 26. Nf4 Ne4 27. Rg2 h4 28. Bd3 hxg3 29. hxg3 Rh8 30. g4 Rh1+ 31. Ke2 Rh4 32. Bxe4 fxe4 { Key: 32.Bxe4 fxe4: knight against bishop with rooks. Black's e-pawn is isolated and the knight will hunt it. } 33. f3 e3 34. Nd3 Bd4 35. c3 Bb6 36. Ne5 Rh1 37. Nc4 Ba7 38. Nxe3 { Key: 38.Nxe3: the pawn falls. White is a pawn up with rook and knight against rook and bishop. } 38... b5 39. g5 Kd7 40. Nf1 Kd6 41. a4 bxa4 42. Rg4 a3 43. bxa3 a5 44. Rf4 Bc5 45. Nd2 Ke7 46. Ra4 Rg1 47. Ne4 Bb6 48. Rc4 Kd7 49. Nf6+ Kd8 50. Ng4 Rg2+ 51. Kd3 Rg3 52. Ke4 Rg1 53. Rc6 Bc7 54. Ra6 Kd7 55. Ra8 Ra1 56. Nf6+ Kc6 57. Rf8 Re1+ 58. Kd3 Bf4 59. Ne4 Kb5 { Key: 59...Kb5? 59...Rg1 60.Rxf7 Bxg5 was the defence (engine +1.6 against +3.0). } 60. Rxf7 e5 61. g6 { Key: 60.Rxf7 e5 61.g6: the passed g-pawn, escorted by the knight, decides. } 61... Re3+ 62. Kc2 Re2+ 63. Kd1 Rg2 64. g7 Kc4 65. Nf6 1-0

