[Why "The first game of the 1927 match and Alekhine's first win against Capablanca. A quiet French with symmetrical pawns turns on one tactical oversight, 16.Rac1, which lets Black win a pawn with 16...Nxc2. Alekhine then keeps the initiative with rook and queen against the white king; Capablanca's counterplay down the e-file comes up a tempo short."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. exd5 exd5 5. Bd3 Nc6 6. Ne2 Nge7 7. O-O Bf5 8. Bxf5 Nxf5 9. Qd3 Qd7 10. Nd1 O-O 11. Ne3 Nxe3 12. Bxe3 Rfe8 13. Nf4 Bd6 14. Rfe1 Nb4 15. Qb3 Qf5 16. Rac1 { Key: 16.Rac1? lets 16...Nxc2 17.Rxc2 Qxf4 win a pawn; 16.Nd3 was needed (engine -0.8 against -1.9). } 16... Nxc2 17. Rxc2 Qxf4 18. g3 Qf5 19. Rce2 b6 20. Qb5 h5 21. h4 Re4 22. Bd2 { Key: 22.Bd2? 22.Bf4 kept the losses small (engine -1.4 against -2.5). } 22... Rxd4 23. Bc3 Rd3 24. Be5 Rd8 25. Bxd6 Rxd6 26. Re5 Qf3 27. Rxh5 Qxh5 { Key: 27.Rxh5 Qxh5 28.Re8+ Kh7 29.Qxd3+: White wins the rook back. The engine preferred 27...Rh6 (-1.5 against -0.4). } 28. Re8+ Kh7 29. Qxd3+ Qg6 30. Qd1 Re6 31. Ra8 Re5 32. Rxa7 c5 33. Rd7 Qe6 34. Qd3+ g6 35. Rd8 d4 36. a4 { Key: 36.a4? 36.Kg2 was the defence (engine -1.8 against -4.0). } 36... Re1+ { Key: 36...Re1+: rook and queen invade around the white king. } 37. Kg2 Qc6+ 38. f3 Re3 39. Qd1 Qe6 40. g4 Re2+ 41. Kh3 Qe3 42. Qh1 Qf4 43. h5 Rf2 0-1

