[Level "5"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Sämisch Variation"]
[Why "The Carlsbad pawn structure and its standard plan, the minority attack: White pushes a- and b-pawns against Black's a-, b- and c-pawns to leave Black a weak pawn on c6. Alekhine gets the weak pawn, but the plan costs time, and Lasker uses it to turn his bishops and pawns against the white king. A plan that wins a pawn on one wing can lose the game on the other."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Nbd7 5. cxd5 exd5 { Key: 5.cxd5 exd5: the Carlsbad structure. White's typical plan is the minority attack with b4 and b5. } 6. Bf4 c6 7. e3 Nh5 8. Bd3 Nxf4 9. exf4 Bd6 10. g3 O-O 11. O-O Re8 12. Qc2 Nf8 13. Nd1 f6 { Key: 13...f6 is slow; 13...Bg4 was the engine's choice (-0.7 against +0.3). } 14. Ne3 Be6 15. Nh4 Bc7 16. b4 { Key: 16.b4: the minority attack starts: two white pawns against three black ones. } 16... Bb6 17. Nf3 Bf7 18. b5 Bh5 19. g4 Bf7 20. bxc6 Rc8 21. Qb2 bxc6 { Key: 20.bxc6 Rc8 21.Qb2 bxc6: the minority attack has done its job: Black's c6 pawn has no pawn beside it. But Black's pieces are now on the kingside. } 22. f5 Qd6 23. Ng2 Bc7 24. Rfe1 h5 { Key: 24...h5: Black hits back on the kingside, where White has pushed his g- and f-pawns (engine -1.4). } 25. h3 Nh7 26. Rxe8+ Rxe8 27. Re1 Rb8 28. Qc1 Ng5 29. Ne5 fxe5 30. Qxg5 e4 { Key: 29.Ne5 fxe5 30.Qxg5 e4: Black's centre pawns roll forward (engine -3.4). } 31. f6 g6 32. f4 hxg4 33. Be2 gxh3 34. Bh5 { Key: 34.Bh5? 34.Ne3 was more stubborn (engine -4.2 against -5.2). } 34... Rb2 35. Nh4 Qxf4 36. Qxf4 Bxf4 0-1

[Event "Semmering"]
[Site "Semmering"]
[Date "1926"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Richard Reti"]
[Black "Savielly Tartakower"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A13"]
[Themes "opposite-bishops,endgame"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "English Opening: Agincourt Defense"]
[Why "Bishops of opposite colours are drawish, but not always. After a mutual oversight on move 26 (White hangs his queen and Black does not take it), Reti wins a pawn and reaches an ending with bishops of opposite colours and pawns on both wings. The black king cannot be on both wings at once, and a second passed pawn decides."]

1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 e6 3. g3 dxc4 4. Qa4+ Bd7 5. Qxc4 c5 6. Ne5 Nc6 7. Nxd7 Qxd7 8. Bg2 Nf6 9. d3 Be7 10. O-O Nd4 11. Nc3 Rd8 12. a4 Nd5 13. e3 Nb6 14. Qa2 Nc2 15. Rb1 Nb4 16. Qb3 Nxd3 17. a5 c4 18. Qd1 Nd5 19. Qg4 O-O 20. Qxc4 Nxc1 21. Rbxc1 Nxc3 22. Rxc3 b5 23. axb6 axb6 24. Rfc1 Bf6 25. R3c2 b5 26. Qc3 Qd3 { Key: 26.Qc3? puts the queen where 26...Bxc3 27.Rxc3 wins it for a bishop (engine -4.8). Tartakower missed it: 26...Qd3. } 27. Qb4 g6 28. Bf1 Qd5 29. Bg2 Qh5 30. Bf1 Rd1 31. Rc8 Rxc8 32. Rxc8+ Bd8 33. Rb8 Kg7 34. Kg2 Qd5+ 35. e4 Qd4 36. Qxd4+ Rxd4 { Key: 35.e4 Qd4 36.Qxd4+ Rxd4: queens off; rooks and bishops of opposite colours. } 37. Bxb5 { Key: 37.Bxb5 wins a pawn. } 37... Be7 38. Rb7 Kf8 39. Kf3 f5 40. exf5 gxf5 41. b3 e5 42. Bc4 f4 43. Rb5 Bd6 44. Rd5 e4+ 45. Kg2 Rxd5 46. Bxd5 e3 { Key: 45...Rxd5 46.Bxd5: only bishops of opposite colours are left, with a pawn more for White. Usually a draw, but White has pawns on both wings. } 47. fxe3 fxe3 48. Kf3 Bc5 49. Bc4 h5 50. Ke4 h4 51. g4 Ke7 52. Kd5 Ba7 53. h3 Kd7 54. b4 Bb8 { Key: 54...Bb8: engine +3.4. The black king guards one wing, the bishop the other, and both are stretched. } 55. Ke4 Bd6 56. b5 Bc5 57. Kf4 Bb6 58. g5 Kd6 59. Kg4 Kc5 60. Be2 Bd8 61. Kxh4 Kd4 62. Kh5 Ke5 63. g6 { Key: 63.g6: the second passed pawn decides. } 1-0

[Event "Hastings"]
[Site "Hastings"]
