[Why "Queens come off on move 13, and the game becomes a lesson in pawn breaks. Pillsbury creates a protected passed pawn on c5, opens a second front with 27.f5, and then breaks with 32.e4 and 33.d5+ to make more passed pawns than the black king can stop. Passed pawns on two wings beat a king that has to watch both."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. e3 g6 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. Nf3 Nf6 6. Bd3 O-O 7. Ne5 dxc4 8. Bxc4 Nd5 9. f4 Be6 10. Qb3 b5 11. Bxd5 Bxd5 12. Nxd5 Qxd5 13. Qxd5 cxd5 { Key: 13.Qxd5 cxd5: queens off. The fight is now about pawn structure and the king's route to the centre. } 14. Nd3 Nd7 15. Bd2 Rfc8 16. Ke2 e6 17. Rhc1 Bf8 18. Rxc8 Rxc8 19. Rc1 Rxc1 20. Bxc1 Bd6 21. Bd2 Kf8 22. Bb4 Ke7 23. Bc5 a6 24. b4 f6 25. g4 Bxc5 26. bxc5 { Key: 25...Bxc5 26.bxc5: White has a protected passed pawn on c5. No black pawn can stop it, and the pawn on d4 defends it. } 26... Nb8 27. f5 { Key: 27.f5! A second front on the kingside, so the black king cannot both watch the c-pawn and guard e6 (engine +2.1). } 27... g5 28. Nb4 a5 { Key: 28...a5? 28...Kd7 29.fxe6+ held on longer (engine +2.8 against +3.6). } 29. c6 Kd6 30. fxe6 Nxc6 31. Nxc6 Kxc6 32. e4 dxe4 33. d5+ { Key: 32.e4 dxe4 33.d5+: two pawn breaks in a row; the d-pawn becomes a second passed pawn. } 33... Kd6 34. Ke3 b4 35. Kxe4 a4 36. Kd4 h5 37. gxh5 a3 38. Kc4 f5 39. h6 f4 40. h7 { Key: 40.h7: a third passed pawn, and it queens. } 1-0

