[Why "Pillsbury gives a rook for a knight to get a knight on d5 and attacking chances, the classic exchange sacrifice. The engine never quite trusts it (Black stays about one pawn better), and Maroczy, a great defender, trades queens and wins the ending with rook against bishop: the rook attacks pawns on both wings, the bishop can guard only one colour."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Bd3 Nc6 6. Be3 a6 7. O-O Be7 8. Nc3 Qc7 9. Qe2 d6 10. Rad1 O-O 11. f4 { Key: 11.f4? Nxd4 12.Bxd4 e5 gains time for Black; 11.Nxc6 was level (engine +0.5 against -0.7). } 11... Nxd4 12. Bxd4 e5 13. fxe5 dxe5 14. Rxf6 { Key: 14.Rxf6! Bxf6 15.Nd5: Pillsbury gives the exchange to remove a defender and plant a knight on d5. } 14... Bxf6 15. Nd5 Qd6 16. Bc3 Bd8 17. Qf2 Re8 18. Bb4 Qg6 19. c4 Be6 20. Bc5 a5 21. Bc2 Bd7 22. Re1 Ra6 23. Be3 b5 24. c5 Bc6 25. Rd1 Ra7 26. Bd3 Qg4 27. Rd2 f5 28. exf5 Rd7 { Key: Moves 27 to 29 trade small errors, each worth about one pawn by the engine: with unequal material both sides misjudge. } 29. Be2 Qh4 30. Nc3 Rxd2 31. Bxd2 Qxf2+ 32. Kxf2 { Key: 30...Rxd2 31.Bxd2 Qxf2+ 32.Kxf2: queens off. Black has the exchange for a pawn. } 32... Be7 33. Be3 Rc8 34. Nxb5 Bxb5 35. Bxb5 Bxc5 36. Bc4+ Kf8 37. b3 Ke7 38. Bxc5+ Rxc5 { Key: 38.Bxc5+ Rxc5: rook against bishop. The rook attacks pawns on both wings; the bishop can guard only one colour. } 39. Ke3 h5 40. g3 Kf6 41. Ke4 h4 42. a4 h3 { Key: 42...h3: the h-pawn ties the bishop down (engine -3.9). } 43. Kd3 Kxf5 44. Kc3 Ke4 45. b4 axb4+ 46. Kxb4 Kd4 47. Bf1 Rc2 48. Bxh3 Rxh2 49. Bf1 Rb2+ 50. Ka3 Rf2 51. Bb5 Rf3+ 52. Kb4 Rxg3 53. a5 Rf3 54. a6 e4 55. Be2 Rf8 56. Kb3 Ke3 57. Bc4 Kd2 58. a7 e3 0-1

