[Round "6"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "Mikhail Chigorin"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D02"]
[Themes "opposite-bishops,endgame"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: London System"]
[Why "Lasker gets a dark-squared bishop on e5, a square the black pawns can no longer attack, against a light-squared bishop. Bishops of opposite colours with rooks still on: the attacker's bishop has no rival. The queens come off on move 43 and Lasker wins the rook ending with bishops after a late slip by Chigorin."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Nbd7 4. Nc3 c6 5. e3 e6 6. Bd3 Bb4 7. O-O h6 8. Qe2 O-O 9. e4 Bxc3 10. bxc3 dxe4 11. Bxe4 Nxe4 12. Qxe4 Nf6 13. Qd3 Nd5 14. Bd2 Ne7 15. Rfe1 Ng6 16. Ne5 Nxe5 17. Rxe5 f6 18. Re3 f5 { Key: 18...f5: Black's e6 pawn is backward and e5 is a hole for White's pieces. } 19. Rg3 Qh4 20. Rh3 Qe7 21. Re1 Rf6 22. Bf4 Bd7 23. Rg3 Be8 24. c4 Qd7 25. Rge3 b6 26. Be5 { Key: 26.Be5: the bishop occupies the hole. White's dark-squared bishop has no rival. } 26... Rg6 27. f3 Bf7 28. Qa3 b5 29. cxb5 { Key: 29.cxb5 releases the tension; 29.Qa6 was stronger (engine +1.6 against +0.7). } 29... cxb5 30. Rc3 Rc8 31. Ree3 Rc4 32. Rxc4 bxc4 33. Qb4 Qd8 34. Qxc4 Qa5 35. Rc3 Kh7 36. h3 Be8 37. a3 Bb5 38. Qb4 Qa6 39. Rc7 Bc6 40. c4 Bxf3 41. g3 Qb6 42. Kf2 Be4 43. Qxb6 axb6 { Key: 43.Qxb6 axb6: queens off; rooks and bishops of opposite colours. } 44. a4 h5 45. h4 Rg4 46. Ke3 Bg2 47. Kd3 Kg8 48. Kc3 Kf8 49. Bf4 Ke8 50. Ra7 g5 51. Bxg5 Rxg3+ 52. Kb4 Rxg5 { Key: 52...Rxg5? 52...Rg4 was the defence (engine +1.4 against +3.6). } 53. hxg5 h4 54. Rh7 h3 55. g6 1-0

[Event "World Championship match, game 4"]
[Site "Moscow"]
[Date "1896"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C64"]
[Themes "knight-vs-bishop,iqp,endgame,world-championship"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Classical Variation, Central Variation"]
[Why "Lasker plays with an isolated queen's pawn, trades queens, and gets a knight against a bishop with a perfect square on c5: Black has no b- or d-pawn left to attack it. The knight and the active rook tie Black down while the white king walks to a7 and frees the a-pawn."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Bc5 4. c3 Nge7 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Bxd2+ 8. Qxd2 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 { Key: 8...d5 9.exd5 Nxd5: White has an isolated d-pawn on d4. } 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. O-O O-O 12. Nc3 f6 13. Rfe1 Rb8 14. h3 Bf5 15. Rac1 Qd6 16. Nh4 Bd7 17. Ne4 Qf4 18. Qxf4 Nxf4 19. Nc5 Bc8 20. b3 Kf7 21. Nf3 Re8 22. Rxe8 Kxe8 23. Re1+ Kf7 24. Nd2 Ne6 25. Nxe6 Bxe6 { Key: 25.Nxe6 Bxe6: knight against bishop with rooks. White's knight will settle on c5, where no black pawn can attack it. } 26. Ne4 Bd5 27. Nc5 Rb4 28. Rd1 Ke7 29. f3 Kd6 30. Kf2 Rb8 31. Re1 Bf7 32. Ne4+ Kd7 33. Ke3 Bd5 34. Nc5+ Kd6 35. Kd3 h5 36. h4 Rh8 37. Kc3 Rb8 38. f4 Rg8 39. g3 g5 40. fxg5 fxg5 41. Re5 gxh4 42. gxh4 Rg3+ 43. Kb4 Rg4 44. Nb7+ Kd7 45. Rxh5 Rxd4+ 46. Ka5 Bf7 { Key: 46...Bf7? 46...c5 was the defence (engine +0.4 against +1.2). } 47. Rh6 Rd2 48. Nc5+ Ke7 49. a4 Rd6 50. Rh8 Rd5 51. b4 Be8 52. Rh6 Rf5 { Key: 52...Rf5? 52...Rd6 was more stubborn (engine +2.7 against +3.6). } 53. Re6+ Kd8 54. Re4 Bf7 55. Ka6 Bd5 56. Rd4 Kc8 57. Kxa7 { Key: 57.Kxa7: the white king has walked to a7 and the a-pawn is free. } 57... Rh5 58. Rf4 Rh8 59. h5 Ba2 60. h6 Bd5 61. h7 Ba2 62. Re4 Bf7 63. Rh4 Ba2 64. Ne4 Bb3 65. a5 Bc2 66. Rg4 1-0

[Event "World Championship match, game 2"]
[Site "Germany"]
[Date "1908"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Siegbert Tarrasch"]
[Black "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C66"]
[Themes "defence,initiative,world-championship"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Berlin Defense, Hedgehog Variation"]
[Why "Lasker the defender. He accepts a worse position, lets Tarrasch take a pawn on a7, and uses the time to bring his dark-squared bishop, rooks and pawns against the white king. Tarrasch misses the moment to consolidate (27.Rd3 instead of 27.Nf5), and 40.a4 loses at once. Counterattack is often the best defence."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O d6 5. d4 Bd7 6. Nc3 Be7 7. Re1 exd4 8. Nxd4 O-O 9. Nxc6 Bxc6 10. Bxc6 bxc6 11. Ne2 Qd7 12. Ng3 Rfe8 13. b3 Rad8 14. Bb2 Ng4 15. Bxg7 Nxf2 16. Kxf2 Kxg7 { Key: 14...Ng4 15.Bxg7 Nxf2 16.Kxf2 Kxg7: a double trade that leaves both kings open. The engine prefers 14...Qe6 (+0.9 against +1.8). } 17. Nf5+ Kh8 18. Qd4+ f6 19. Qxa7 { Key: 19.Qxa7 wins a pawn; Black's compensation is the dark-squared bishop and the open white king. } 19... Bf8 20. Qd4 Re5 21. Rad1 Rde8 22. Qc3 Qf7 23. Ng3 Bh6 24. Qf3 d5 25. exd5 Be3+ 26. Kf1 cxd5 27. Rd3 { Key: 27.Rd3? 27.Nf5 kept White ahead (engine +1.3 against -0.5). } 27... Qe6 28. Re2 f5 29. Rd1 f4 30. Nh1 d4 31. Nf2 Qa6 32. Nd3 Rg5 33. Ra1 Qh6 34. Ke1 Qxh2 35. Kd1 Qg1+ 36. Ne1 Rge5 { Key: 36...Rge5? 36...d3 was stronger (engine -0.9 against -2.3). } 37. Qc6 R5e6 { Key: 37...R5e6 gives some back; 37...Kg8 kept the pressure (engine -2.4 against -0.9). } 38. Qxc7 R8e7 39. Qd8+ Kg7 40. a4 { Key: 40.a4? 40.Qc8 was the defence (engine -1.6 against -4.2). } 40... f3 41. gxf3 Bg5 0-1

[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York"]
[Date "1924"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Alexander Alekhine"]
[Black "Richard Reti"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "E62"]
[Themes "bishop-pair,positional"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Fianchetto Variation, Carlsbad Variation"]
[Why "Alekhine accepts doubled f-pawns for the bishop pair, uses the doubled pawn to control e5, wins a pawn on the queenside and then opens the kingside for the bishops. The engine's advantage grows from +0.6 to more than +4 with no sacrifice: two bishops on an open board."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nc3 d6 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. d5 Nb8 8. O-O Bg4 9. h3 Bxf3 10. exf3 e6 { Key: 9.h3 Bxf3 10.exf3: White takes doubled f-pawns for the bishop pair. } 11. f4 { Key: 11.f4: the doubled pawn advances and controls e5 and g5. } 11... exd5 12. cxd5 c5 13. dxc6 Nxc6 14. Be3 Qd7 15. Qa4 Rac8 16. Rad1 b6 17. b3 Rfd8 18. Rd3 Ne7 19. Nb5 d5 20. Nxa7 Ra8 21. Bxb6 { Key: 20.Nxa7 Ra8 21.Bxb6: White wins a pawn with the bishops' help. } 21... Qxa4 22. bxa4 Rd7 23. Nb5 Rxa4 24. Nc3 Ra6 25. Rb1 Rb7 26. Bc5 Rxb1+ 27. Nxb1 Nc6 28. Nc3 Ra5 29. Bb6 Ra6 30. Bc5 Ra5 31. Be3 Nb4 32. Rd2 h6 33. a4 Ne4 { Key: 33...Ne4? 33...h5 was the defence (engine +1.8 against +2.7). } 34. Nxe4 dxe4 35. Rd8+ Kh7 36. Bxe4 Rxa4 37. f5 Ra6 38. h4 h5 39. g4 { Key: 39.g4 opens the kingside for the bishops; 39.Rd7 was even stronger (engine +4.0 against +2.9). } 39... Ra5 40. fxg6+ fxg6 41. gxh5 Rxh5 42. Bg5 Bc3 43. Rd7+ Kg8 44. Bxg6 1-0
